From: Allan Ortiz <aortez@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] watchdog: bcm2835_wdt: clamp the hardware timeout

The watchdog core accepts virtual timeouts above the hardware range when
max_hw_heartbeat_ms is provided. bcm2835_wdt_start still masks the virtual
seconds into the 20-bit register, so 210 seconds programs only 2 seconds.
The core's roughly 8-second ping interval then cannot prevent a reset.

Clamp the register timeout to its largest whole-second value and advertise
the same 15-second hardware heartbeat. Keep the requested virtual timeout
unchanged so the core can provide the full userspace deadline.

Confirmed on a Raspberry Pi 5 with the pinned 6.6.63 kernel: SETTIMEOUT and
GETTIMEOUT report 210 while GETTIMELEFT immediately reports 2.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Allan Ortiz <aortez@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/watchdog/bcm2835_wdt.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/bcm2835_wdt.c
@@ -36,7 +36,6 @@
 
 #define SECS_TO_WDOG_TICKS(x) ((x) << 16)
 #define WDOG_TICKS_TO_SECS(x) ((x) >> 16)
-#define WDOG_TICKS_TO_MSECS(x) ((x) * 1000 >> 16)
 
 struct bcm2835_wdt {
 	void __iomem		*base;
@@ -60,12 +59,14 @@
 static int bcm2835_wdt_start(struct watchdog_device *wdog)
 {
 	struct bcm2835_wdt *wdt = watchdog_get_drvdata(wdog);
+	u32 timeout = min_t(u32, wdog->timeout,
+			    WDOG_TICKS_TO_SECS(PM_WDOG_TIME_SET));
 	uint32_t cur;
 	unsigned long flags;
 
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&wdt->lock, flags);
 
-	writel_relaxed(PM_PASSWORD | (SECS_TO_WDOG_TICKS(wdog->timeout) &
+	writel_relaxed(PM_PASSWORD | (SECS_TO_WDOG_TICKS(timeout) &
 				PM_WDOG_TIME_SET), wdt->base + PM_WDOG);
 	cur = readl_relaxed(wdt->base + PM_RSTC);
 	writel_relaxed(PM_PASSWORD | (cur & PM_RSTC_WRCFG_CLR) |
@@ -158,7 +159,7 @@
 	.info =		&bcm2835_wdt_info,
 	.ops =		&bcm2835_wdt_ops,
 	.min_timeout =	1,
-	.max_hw_heartbeat_ms =	WDOG_TICKS_TO_MSECS(PM_WDOG_TIME_SET),
+	.max_hw_heartbeat_ms =	WDOG_TICKS_TO_SECS(PM_WDOG_TIME_SET) * 1000,
 	.timeout =	WDOG_TICKS_TO_SECS(PM_WDOG_TIME_SET),
 };
 
